>other carrier. (Fedex, UPS and Checkfree provide different services).
>    This looks more like an extortion to me than a free contract.

"am not allowed"? "extortion"?

Perry writes:

>good conduct -- that is to say, that the action, if taken deliberately
>with the notion that one was attempting to undermine the postal
>monopoly, might be a "good" thing (for my personal value of good) and
>might not indicate a tendancy to try to violate contracts.

What postal monopoly? Have I missed something in this discussion? Is
there a law that prohibits the existing commercial mail-carriers or
some start-up company from delivering letter-sized mail? Although the
word "monopoly" *can* mean simply exclusive ownership, I was under the
impression that the more rigorous economics-based definition was more
something on the order of:

exclusive ownership through legal privilege, command of supply, or
concerted action [courtesy my on-line Websters]

Does the USPS qualify for any of this?

legal priviledge - As I said, there are certainly many commercial carriers
that deliver large packages and I am not aware of any law that prohibits
them from doing the same with smaller ones - its just that it's apparently
not economical for them to do so.

command of supply - Envelopes, mail trucks, stamps are all easy to make.

concerted action - Well, much as my perverted little brain can delight in
images of phalanxes of "disgruntled postal workers" blowing away the
competition in death-dealing highway duels and running over any old ladies
foolish enough to try to drop a letter into the Acme Mail Company's box on
the corner, I suspect it ain't gonna happen.

No one is going to stop you if use UPS to deliver all your letters in a
cardboard box.

Don't mistake the above to mean that I think that the Feds *should* be
delivering mail. I don't. I'm still not sure just *what*, if anything,
I think the government should be doing (but I suspect it has something
to do with that ring around my toiletbowl and the mess in the kitchen).
Its just that I don't think that the USPS is the same as the IRS. No one  
forces you to use the mail at least once a year. No one arrests you for
not writing your mother more often (much as she might want them to).
Your Friendly Neighborhood Cop (tm) will not show up if you open a corner
franchise of Mailboxes Ad Nauseum. 


It may be a government-owned business but it is a business, and I seems
to me that you are stealing from them when you deliberately avoid paying
them the amount they charge for letter delivery.

I salute your ingenuity though.

Now if I can just figure out how to send my 1040 in with "taxes due" and
no return address and get the IRS to pick up the bill;)

 - Jeff

