I am pretty sure, though not 100%, that other companies are prohibited
from competing with the PO on first class letters.  The argument is
that private companies would "skim the cream" by taking the high-volumen
profitable routes (that are being used to subsidzie the low-volume 
money-losing routes).  Using the profitable routes to subsidize the
unprofitable ones is what allows us to send any letter for the same
price.  This is not something that we would expect under a competitive
system, I would guess.

If you believe that companies could legally compete with the post office
but have not found it economical to do so, this is tantamount to saying
that the PO is offering that service at a lower price than what private
companies could charge.  For this to happen, either the PO would have
to be more efficient than private companies (ha!) or heavilty subsidizing
first-class mail, which I don't think they are doing.  They are cross-
subsizing first class, so that some routes are cheaper than they might
be, but overall I don't think that they are subsidizing it.

Robert


