customers--are clearly "subsidizing" the "expensive"
routes--Kalispell, Montana to Aptos, California, for example.

This is a very competitive market and the carriers have chosen this
pricing strategy for various reasons. UPS charges approximately by
distance, but not by fine-grained calculations of "difficulty
distance."

(I don't believe rates are set by the gub'ment, or that "price
discrimination" is banned, but I could be wrong. I think the flat
rates are set for market reasons.)

It is quite possible, even likely, that private competitors to the
Post Awful could choose to have flat rates, for similar reasons.

Rates could actually drop a bit, because wages/benefits would be set
by markets, not by the Lettercarriers Union, large anachronistic
offices would vanish, and personal mail would not subsidize
Congressional mail, AIDS Awareness brochures, and "junk" mail (*).

(*) Many claim "junk" mail actually subsidizes the Postal Service. It
is hard for me to imagine that shipping a pound of junk mail for less
than a first class stamp is somehow making letter delivery cheaper.

In any case, the issue is becoming moot for many folks like us. Bills
can be paid electronically, e-mail is the preferred communication
medium, and important documents are either faxed or FedExed.

-Tim May



