From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: SONG AND STORY:  Extropian music and science fiction

    Kennita asked about Extropian music.  The Moody Blues's "To Our
Children's Children's Children" (about 1970 -- now in press as CD
audio), has several likely cuts, and the opening track is a
not-too-bad version of what a Saturn launch sounds like.

    Sample lyrics, as I remember them:

        Blasting, billowing, bursting forth
	With the power of ten billion butterfly sneezes,
	Man, with his flaming pyres
	Has conquered the wayward breezes.

        Climbing to Tranquillity, far above the clouds,
        Conceiving the heavens, clear of misty shrouds,
        Higher and higher and higher.
                      .  .  .

        Vast vision must improve our sight,
        Perhaps at last we'll find an end to our home's endless night,
	And the beginning of true worth ...
                      .  .  .

        Such a lot of space for me and you ...
                      .  .  .

        I never thought I'd get to be a million ...

    But not all is Extropian, for the album seems to be libretto for
Olaf Stapledon's _Starmaker_ (1950s (?) science fiction novel), which
contains -- like the album -- a mixture of ideas Extropians will find
frustrating.  Stapledon described several Extropian themes (notably
uploading and augmented intelligence) a decade or more before the
authors usually mentioned here, including an uploaded entity that
would lose Vinge's transcend in the noise.  Yet his implementation is
quite stateist, and his entities' purposes and activities are fraught
with theology.  Still, Extropian historians (an oxymoron?) will find
this work an interesting antecedent of several familiar ideas.

    An antedote is Fritz Leiber's _Wanderer_ (I can never remember if
that's L-e-i- or L-i-e-), a Hugo-winning novel in the late 1960s,
perhaps best described as "Kimball Kinnison fights the Transcend Tac
Squad", and a clear riposte to Stapledon's world view.  Freedom-loving
aliens pop from hyperspace right here in the solar system, hotly
pursued and low on gas, and notice that Luna is just right to top off
their tanks.  By oversight they fail to adjust their continuum
twiddlers to mask their vehicle's gravitation, so Terran tides
increase by the ratio (dirigible planet mass)/(lunar mass).  Humans
hide and try to survive; a few meet the aliens.  The end has a "Get
the state off my back!  Off the pigs!" note that many will find
familiar.  A sideline is the entertaining prospect that
super-intellects may have super-hangups.

    Present company excepted, of course.

                                           -- Jay Freeman

